Of course, the standard response would be, "Where do you draw the line?  Is
it ok if the binary is <10K?  20K?  What if it's only three bytes over?",
etc.  Granted, some newsservers probably already have their own limits in,
but that's a per-server basis, rather than a newsgroup one.

To remain brief: I know of no news server which would impose a binary-size
limit on any *.binaries.* group, since that'd make very little sense.  There
are many, however, which choose to simply not carry them at all (the one I
use being a prime example).  This, combined with the fact that news servers
can impose arbitrary binary-size limits on non-binary groups, indicates
farily strongly to me that, if you want your post to be visible to the
entire newsgroup, you should not include binaries unless you're in one of
the aforementioned *.binaries.* groups.  Of course, if you don't want your
post to be fully visible, then you should just e-mail the people you want to
see it.  :-)

Whether or not this is official, well-known usenet protocol (many have
posted reasons for it being the former; the latter is harder to prove) is
irrelevant--it flows logically from how news servers are set up most
commonly.  (Side example: when I'm driving, even if I haven't seen a speed
limit sign, I tend to follow a: the speed the other cars are travelling at
and b: the speed I've driven on similar roads before, rather than assuming
there is no speed limit.  Of course, there are roads which have no speed
limit, such as the autobahn, but I'd probably know if I were there.  If,
instead, I were to assume, contrary to my past experience and the evidence
of the other drivers, that there was no speed limit, I'd have no basis to
complain when I got pulled over.)

